---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'FACTORS', title: 'Google’s three factors', text: 'Google ranks the map by relevance, distance and prominence. You can’t touch distance, so all the work is in the other two.' },
  { label: 'CATEGORY', title: 'Primary category first', text: 'The first field that tells Google what you do. If it’s wrong, the effort you put into reviews and posts doesn’t pay off.' },
  { label: 'MEASUREMENT', title: 'Your phone misleads you', text: 'The ranking you see from your office is your best spot. The real picture emerges when you measure from different points across your area.' },
  { label: 'TIMING', title: 'The order of the work', text: 'We can’t give you a timeframe. What we can tell you is the order: measure first, fix the root problems, then produce.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'etkenler', label: 'What decides rankings?' },
  { id: 'olcum', label: 'Where do you really rank?' },
  { id: 'sira', label: 'What order to work in' },
  { id: 'alaka', label: 'How relevance grows' },
  { id: 'bilinirlik', label: 'How prominence grows' },
  { id: 'yaramayanlar', label: 'Tactics that don’t work' },
  { id: 'sure', label: 'How long does it take?' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Can you buy a ranking on Google Maps?',
    a: 'No. The positions of the three businesses on the map aren’t for sale; the only place you can pay for is the ad space marked “Sponsored”. Anyone selling a ranking guarantee isn’t relying on any agreement with Google.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will posting updates raise my ranking?',
    a: 'Not on its own. Posts show that the profile is active, and they don’t make up for a wrong category or missing reviews. Once the basics are in place, they take their place as part of a regular rhythm.',
  },
  {
    q: 'My competitor added keywords to their business name. What can I do?',
    a: 'You can report it to Google. Open that business’s listing on the map, suggest an edit and enter its real name. Google decides whether to accept it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'My competitor is ahead with fake reviews. What should I do?',
    a: 'Report the reviews that break the rules to Google, and keep working while you wait for the outcome. The reporting process isn’t guaranteed and can take weeks. The lasting defense is growing your own flow of real reviews.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="guides/rank-higher"
  title="How to Rank Higher on Google Maps | Wiserrise"
  description="How to rank higher on Google Maps: the factors that decide rankings, why your own phone misleads you and the order in which to work."
  eyebrow="GUIDE · MAP RANKINGS"
  h1="How do you rank higher on Google Maps?"
  crumb="Ranking higher"
  lede="You rank higher on the map by telling Google clearly what you do and becoming more prominent than your competitors. Google ranks by relevance, distance and prominence; you can’t change distance, so the work is in the other two. Start with measurement across your area, then work through category, profile, reviews and website in that order."
  schemaType="article"
  datePublished="2026-08-01"
  dateModified="2026-11-06"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Guides', href: p('guides') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'How do you get more Google reviews?', href: p('guides/more-reviews'), text: 'How to grow your review volume without running into the rules.' },
    { label: 'Business not showing on Google', href: p('guides/not-showing'), text: 'If you don’t appear on the map at all, rule out the causes one by one.' },
    { label: 'Map Rank Tracking', href: p('services/rank-tracking'), text: 'We measure where you rank from different points around you.' },
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo'), text: 'The service where we fix your Google Business Profile and your website together.' },
  ]}
  relatedHeading="Related pages"
  ctaLabel="Get a free analysis"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="etkenler">What decides rankings on Google Maps?</h2>

  <p>
    Google explains this itself: local results are ranked by relevance, distance and prominence. These are the terms Google
    uses in its own help pages. It weighs all three together and doesn’t disclose the weights.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Factor</th>
          <th>What it means</th>
          <th>In your hands?</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Relevance</strong></td>
          <td>Whether your business does what the searcher is looking for</td>
          <td>Yes. Category, service list, the pages on your website.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Distance</strong></td>
          <td>How close you are to the searcher or to the neighborhood being searched</td>
          <td>No. Google calculates it from the searcher’s location.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Prominence</strong></td>
          <td>How well known your business is</td>
          <td>Yes. Reviews, mentions on other websites, the strength of your website.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    You can’t touch distance. All the work is in getting ahead of your competitors on relevance and prominence.
  </p>

  <p>
    The most common mistake we see is pushing on only one of the two. The business with the wrong category keeps
    collecting reviews, and the business with a quarter of its competitors’ reviews posts updates every day.
  </p>

  <h2 id="olcum">Where do you really rank right now?</h2>

  <p>
    A single search won’t tell you. There’s no single ranking on the map, because Google calculates it from where the
    searcher is standing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Say you run Eastside Vehicle Inspections in Denver. When you type “vehicle inspection” outside your shop, you rank
    first on the map. When someone living on the other side of the city runs the same search, you’re missing from the top three, and that person
    calls your competitor without ever seeing you.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>A search from your own phone doesn’t count as measurement.</strong> Google personalizes results by your
      location and search history, which makes it easier for you to see your own business. With businesses that rank
      first from the office while the phone stays quiet, this is the first place we look.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    We start with <a href={p('services/rank-tracking')}>measurement across your area</a>. Once you see where you rank from
    different points around you, it becomes clear where you’re strong and where you’re weak. Work done without measuring
    has no clear direction.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sira">In what order should you work?</h2>

  <p>
    Order matters. The lower steps don’t pay off until the ones above them are fixed. We work through every profile in
    this order:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Primary category.</strong> Check the category of the businesses in the top three for your target search. If
      yours is different, this is the first thing to fix.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A complete profile.</strong> Service list and descriptions, photos, opening hours, phone number and website
      link.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Review volume.</strong> If you’re clearly below the competitors in the top three, this is the priority.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A website that supports the profile.</strong> Does every service have its own page, and does the site open
      properly on Google?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Consistency.</strong> Posts, photos and review replies. A rhythm spread over weeks.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    If you want to check the first two steps yourself, our <a href={p('tools/gbp-audit')}>free profile audit tool</a>
    scores your profile in 10 questions and lists the items that come out weak.
  </p>

  <h2 id="alaka">How do you tell Google what you do?</h2>

  <p>
    Relevance is Google’s decision that “this business does what’s being searched for”. We feed that decision from four
    places, starting with the one with the biggest effect:
  </p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>
      <strong>Primary category.</strong> The strongest relevance signal we see in the field. Add secondary categories only
      for services you really provide.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Service list.</strong> Write your services in the words customers search with, and add a short description
      to each. A list left the way Google suggests it is identical to your competitors’.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A separate page for each service on your website.</strong> A service mentioned in three paragraphs on a single
      “our services” page struggles in that service’s searches. We write these pages as part of our
      <a href={p('services/content')}>content production</a> work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The same naming.</strong> If the profile says “root canal treatment” and the website says “endodontics”, the
      match weakens. Use the customer’s word for the service in both places.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="bilinirlik">What increases your prominence?</h2>

  <p>
    Prominence is how well known your business is. Google reads it from everything it finds about you online: links,
    news, directory listings and reviews. Your website’s position in the regular search results counts too.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its most measurable part is reviews. Google openly says that review count and rating affect local rankings. The
    service and neighborhood a customer mentions in a review also tell everyone reading your profile what you do.
  </p>

  <p>
    Besides reviews, there are two more sources. Your name, address and phone number should be written the same way
    everywhere they appear; we explain why on our <a href={p('services/local-seo')}>local SEO page</a>. Mentions on
    industry websites, in local news or in association listings also make it easier for Google to recognize you, because
    prominence is read from exactly these traces.
  </p>

  <p>
    We explain step by step how to grow the review side in our <a href={p('guides/more-reviews')}>reviews guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yaramayanlar">Which tactics don’t work?</h2>

  <p>Tactics that get tried a lot and don’t pay off, some of which also put your profile at risk:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Adding keywords to the business name.</strong> Writing “Eastside Vehicle Inspections Denver Cheapest
      Inspection” instead of “Eastside Vehicle Inspections” breaks Google’s rules. If a competitor reports it, the name gets corrected, and a
      repeat can get the profile suspended.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Buying fake reviews.</strong> Google’s filters catch them, and when they don’t, the customers reading them
      notice. It puts the profile at risk of suspension.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Opening a second profile.</strong> A second profile for a business without a branch breaks the rules. It gets
      flagged as a duplicate, and both get weaker.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Adding unrelated secondary categories.</strong> A category for a service you don’t provide blurs your
      relevance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Only posting updates.</strong> Posting every day while the basics are missing won’t move your ranking.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    We know that adding a neighborhood or service to the business name works in the short term. We still don’t do it,
    because no ranking is worth the risk of getting your profile suspended.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sure">How long does it take to rank higher?</h2>

  <p>
    We can’t give you a timeframe; it depends on where your business starts, on the competition and on the channel. What
    we can tell you is the order of the work: we measure first, fix the root problems, then produce.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the problem comes from a single root mistake like a wrong category, the fix itself is quick. If your profile is in
    good shape and your reviews lag behind your competitors’, the timeline depends directly on how fast you collect
    reviews; in competitive city centers every stage takes a little longer.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same goes for the regular search results below the map, meaning your website’s ranking. You see what we did and
    how the measurement changed in your weekly report.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Where in your area are you <span class="highlight">falling behind</span>?</h2>
    <p>
      The free analysis is a video we record for your business: where you stand today and what to start with first. It
      lands in your inbox within <span style="white-space: nowrap;">24 hours</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
